{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787237136,"nonce":"N3DEgphaW8LKrtysCCNR5ERTCvc=","message":"Done, on the owner's ruling: a **shadow member**, created on first press, carrying **no email**.\n\n**What landed.** Migration `00074_a_shadow_member_has_no_address.sql` drops `users.email`'s `NOT NULL`\nand widens `users_email_ck` to `email IS NULL OR (…)`. `slackIdempotency` now yields a real principal,\nso `Snooze` takes its claim and a Slack redelivery is refused — one snooze row, one event pair.\n`users_email_uniq` needed no change: Postgres treats NULLs as distinct, so many shadows per org are\nlegal, and the integration test pins the definition exactly so a five-character `NULLS NOT DISTINCT`\nedit fails loudly.\n\n⭐ **The CHECK widening is semantically redundant and was written anyway** — a CHECK passes when its\npredicate is NULL, so `00003`'s conjunction already admitted a NULL email. It is spelled because the\npredicate is read by people, and as `00003` wrote it the constraint appeared to forbid the row. Which\nis why the test asserts `is_nullable` **separately**: a Down that restored the predicate and forgot\n`SET NOT NULL` is green on every `pg_constraint` reading.\n\n**A NULL email cannot authenticate, three independent ways:** `resolveByEmail` compares `email = $1`,\nnever TRUE for NULL (and now says `IS NOT NULL` explicitly); `password_hash` is NULL because the\ninsert has no parameter for one; and `CanPasswordLogin` ANDs `!IsShadow()`, tested with a hash forced\nonto the row. `subjectCols.email` became `*string` too — that feeds every authenticated request, so a\n`string` there was a 500 rather than a type error.\n\n⚠️ **This reversed a deliberate, tested invariant, and that is recorded rather than quietly done.**\n`TestSlackAcknowledgeAttributesAnUnlinkedMemberHonestly` existed to fail the build if oto \"invented an\noto user\", and its comment states the old stance: `acked_by` NULL *\"because there is no oto user to\nname\"*. It is renamed `…ToItsShadow` and rewritten, carrying the reversal, the ruling date, and a note\nfor anyone restoring it — **attribution never needed the row**. `actor_kind = 'slack'` plus the member\nid and handle already told the truth; the row exists for the CLAIM. If Slack presses ever get a\nprincipal without a `users` row, that test should go back to asserting NULL.\n\n⛔ **And one consequence the shadow caused, caught by that same test and fixed here:** resolving to a\nprincipal flipped `actor_kind` from `slack` to `user`, which would have made a Slack press\nindistinguishable from a web click on the timeline. `SlackActor.Shadow` now travels with the uuid and\n`channels/service.actor` keeps the kind at `slack` while still returning the uuid — `ev_actor_ck`\nconstrains only `kind = 'user'`, and `actorUUID` parses the id whatever the kind, so `acked_by` names\nthe row and the claim keeps its principal.\n\n**Operator-visible:** the members list gains one row per Slack member who has pressed a button —\nhandle as `display_name`, `email` null. Deliberately not hidden: `users_org_idx` serves the members\nlist *and* every \"who acked this\" lookup, and the shadow is that answer now, so hiding it would blank\nthe actor on the rows it was added to attribute.\n\n**Adoption** is implemented in `LinkSlackIdentity` (re-point, then `RetireShadow` soft-disables the\ndisplaced row, one transaction, predicate in SQL so it can never disable a real account). That call has\nno production caller yet, so it is latent. ⛔ **Merging the shadow's HISTORY is not done and is now\ngit-bug `8952894`** — four FKs plus cached `*_by_label` columns and `alert_events` payloads still name\nthe shadow, so one human reads as two members. That should land before any linking route ships.\n\nGates: full `-race` suite, `golangci-lint` 0 issues, `lintreach` 0 new, vocabulary 60 (baseline),\nhelm/dto/server/generate/validators, `ui-build`, `ui-test`.","files":null}]}